{
  "schemaVersion": 3,
  "id": "overtones",
  "name": "Overtones",
  "tagline": "One pure tone, its overtones blooming slowly.",
  "description": "The bed is a single tone built from the root's harmonic series: octaves and pure fifths, and now and then the natural seventh, each overtone swelling on its own clock, so the colour changes while the pitch never does. The kick and bass are tuned to the same root; nothing in the mix beats against anything else.",
  "use": {
    "name": "Hours",
    "for": "The longest sessions, when small irritations add up.",
    "why": "Tuning lore (432 Hz, 'healing' frequencies) fixes on the wrong number: absolute pitch is a convention, and the ear responds to the ratios between pitches. Equal-tempered chords beat. A major third near middle C beats about ten times a second, a flutter the ear readily hears; faster beating becomes roughness, the acoustic signature of alarms and screams. Here the bed is one tone built from the root's harmonic series at exact ratios, and the kick and bass are tuned to the same root, so nothing beats and the ear hears one tone, not a chord. That consonance follows harmonicity is well established; that a beat-free bed eases the longest sessions is our inference, and untested."
  },
